---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../../../styles/story.css";
import ReadingModes from "../../../components/ReadingModes.astro";
import Cite from "../../../components/Cite.astro";
import SourcedBlock from "../../../components/SourcedBlock.astro";
import StorySources from "../../../components/StorySources.astro";
import StoryState from "../../../components/StoryState.astro";
import StateEvent from "../../../components/StateEvent.astro";
import { readPublishedState, eventPath } from "../../../lib/state.mjs";
import {
  event, talarico, paxton, computed, filing, magaCash, poll, ieGeneral,
  marchNotice, grahamNotice, calendar, tracker, fecDates, delRay, tcm,
  monthlyPayees, usd,
} from "../../../data/texassenate202609.mjs";
const state = readPublishedState().events.get("event-texas-senate-september-2026");
if (!state) throw new Error("Missing publication selection: event-texas-senate-september-2026");
if (state.status === "merged") return Astro.redirect(eventPath(state.survivor), 301);
---
{state.status === "retired" ? <StateEvent view={state} /> : <BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/texas-senate/">The Texas Senate race</a><span>September 2026</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story" id="story-root">
      <p class="story-kicker" data-layer="narrative">MAGA INC. &middot; September 5, 2026</p>
      <h1 data-layer="fact">{event.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">{event.dek}</p>
      <ReadingModes />

      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact">
        <details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-catch">The catch</a></li>
          <li><a href="#where-this-sits">Where this sits</a></li>
          <li><a href="#who-feels-it">Who feels it</a></li>
          <li><a href="#outlets">What the coverage got right, and what it got wrong</a></li>
          <li><a href="#claim-check">Claim checked against the record</a></li>
          <li><a href="#what-happened-next">What happened next</a></li>
          <li><a href="#unknowns">What we do not know yet</a></li>
          <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
        </ol>
      </details></nav>
      <div class="kpi-strip" data-layer="fact">
        {event.kpis.map((k) => (
          <div class="kpi">
            <span class="kpi-value">{k.value}<small>{k.unit ? ` ${k.unit}` : ""}</small></span>
            <span class="kpi-label">{k.label}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <aside class="three-things" data-layer="fact">
        <p class="tt-label">Three things to know</p>
        <ol>
          <li>On September 5, treasurer Charles Gantt signed a 48-hour notice of independent expenditures totaling ${usd(filing.total)} for the Texas Senate general election, the seat now held by Sen. John Cornyn.<Cite s="fec-form24-cover" passage="Date Signed: 09/05/2026" /> The notice lists two ${usd(filing.opposeTalarico)} expenditures to Del Ray Media LLC for connected TV and digital ads: one opposing James Talarico, one supporting Warren Kenneth Paxton Jr.<Cite s="fec-form24-2010907" passage="DEL RAY MEDIA LLC" /></li>
          <li>Texas's general election is November 3, 2026, {computed.daysDisseminationToElection} days after the ads' public date.<Cite s="sos-dates-2026" passage="General election day." /> Talarico's committees reported ${usd(talarico.receipts)} in receipts and ${usd(talarico.disbursements)} in disbursements through June 30.<Cite s="fec-talarico-july-quarterly" passage="$68,555,930.42" /> Paxton's committees reported ${usd(paxton.receipts)} in receipts and ${usd(paxton.disbursements)} in disbursements through the same date.<Cite s="fec-paxton-july-quarterly" passage="$9,248,698.53" /></li>
          <li>A University of Texas / Texas Politics Project poll of {poll.n.toLocaleString("en-US")} registered voters, taken August 5 to 13, found {poll.talarico} percent for Talarico, {poll.paxton} percent for Paxton, and {poll.brown} percent for Libertarian Ted Brown on the trial ballot, the poll's head-to-head vote question.<Cite s="ut-txpp-poll-2026-08" passage="42% of Texas voters preferring Democrat James Talarico and 39% planning to vote for Republican Ken Paxton" /></li>
        </ol>
      </aside>
      <p class="railed rail-note" data-layer="fact">Story updated {event.updated}.</p>

      <div data-layer="proof" class="event-record-context">
        <p class="sources-line">This story rests on MAGA INC.'s September 5 48-hour notice (FEC file {filing.fileNumber}), the committee's August monthly report covering July, the Federal Election Commission's candidate pages for the July quarterlies, the Commission's 2026 independent-expenditure bulk file saved September 8, MAGA INC.'s March 11 notice for a Georgia special election, the Texas Secretary of State's 2026 dates page, November 3 election-law calendar, independent-candidate list, and Candidate Bio Portal capture for the November general, the University of Texas / Texas Politics Project August poll, MAGA Inc. and Ken Paxton's posts on X, and coverage checked against those records. The $10 million figure is the FEC filing. It is not an ad-tracking firm's estimate.</p>
      </div>

      <section id="what-happened">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The filing</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>

        <SourcedBlock source="fec-form24-cover" kind="record">
          <p>MAGA INC., committee {filing.committeeId}, filed a 48-hour notice of independent expenditures, FEC file {filing.fileNumber}. Treasurer Charles Gantt signed it on September 5, 2026.<Cite s="fec-form24-cover" passage="Date Signed: 09/05/2026" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="fec-form24-2010907" kind="record">
          <p>The list of independent expenditures in the notice (Schedule E) lists two expenditures of ${usd(filing.opposeTalarico)} each to Del Ray Media LLC of Alexandria, Virginia. The purpose of both is "PLACED MEDIA: CONNECTED TV & DIGITAL ADVERTISING." The date of disbursement or obligation is September 4. The date of public distribution is September 5. The election is the 2026 general.<Cite s="fec-form24-2010907" passage="PLACED MEDIA: CONNECTED TV & DIGITAL ADVERTISING" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="fec-form24-2010907" kind="record">
          <p>One ${usd(filing.opposeTalarico)} line opposes James Talarico, candidate {filing.talaricoId}, for Senate in Texas. The other ${usd(filing.supportPaxton)} line supports Warren Kenneth Jr. Paxton, candidate {filing.paxtonId}. The notice totals ${usd(filing.total)} for the period.<Cite s="fec-form24-2010907" passage="Total Expenditures This Period = $10000000" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">An independent expenditure is money spent on a message that names a candidate and asks voters to elect or defeat that person, without giving the money to the campaign. MAGA INC. is a hybrid political committee with a non-contribution account: one account limited by contribution caps and one unlimited account used for independent spending. The $10 million here is that kind of spending, not a check to Paxton's campaign committee.<Cite s="fec-maga-inc-committee" passage="Hybrid PAC (with Non-Contribution Account) - Nonqualified" /></p>

        <SourcedBlock source="fec-form24-certification" kind="record">
          <p>On the same notice Gantt certified, "Under penalty of perjury I certify that the independent expenditures reported herein were not made in cooperation, consultation, or concert with, or at the request or suggestion of, any candidate or authorized committee."<Cite s="fec-form24-certification" passage="not made in cooperation, consultation, or concert" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="ap-texas-senate-ad-blitz" kind="checked">
          <p>Alex Pfeiffer, a spokesman for MAGA, Inc., posting the same day, wrote: "High Tax Talarico wants to take money out of Texans' pockets. MAGA Inc. is going to ensure Texas knows about Talarico's radical policies and elects Ken Paxton to the U.S. Senate."<Cite s="ap-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="a spokesman for MAGA" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="magainc-x-paxton-taxes-2026-09-05" kind="record">
          <p>The MAGA Inc. account on X posted an ad whose caption reads, "When it comes to taxes, Ken Paxton is the better choice."<Cite s="magainc-x-paxton-taxes-2026-09-05" passage="When it comes to taxes, Ken Paxton is the better choice." /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="magainc-x-talarico-taxes-2026-09-05" kind="record">
          <p>A second MAGA Inc. ad the same day is captioned, "James Talarico means higher taxes for Texas."<Cite s="magainc-x-talarico-taxes-2026-09-05" passage="James Talarico means higher taxes for Texas." /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The Associated Press writes that the ads portray Talarico "as seeking tax hikes more broadly while claiming that Paxton would give tax breaks to first-time homebuyers and families with children," and that Talarico "has favored higher taxes on wealthier households."<Cite s="ap-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="seeking tax hikes more broadly" /> Fox News writes, "The two anti-Talarico ads slam his policies on taxes and rural hospitals while touting Paxton's support for doubling the child tax credit."<Cite s="fox-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="The two anti-Talarico ads slam his policies on taxes and rural hospitals" /> The Texas Tribune says one ad points to Talarico's vote against the 2019 bill requiring an election to raise property tax revenue by more than 3.5 percent, and that "Talarico’s economic plan includes raising taxes on corporations and billionaires and expanding tax credits for parents and lower-income workers."<Cite s="tribune-maga-inc-2026-09-05" passage="Talarico’s economic plan includes raising taxes on corporations and billionaires" /></p>

        <SourcedBlock source="paxton-x-2026-09-05" kind="record">
          <p>Paxton posted that Talarico "has a record of raising taxes on working families" while his "Protecting the Texas Promise Agenda cuts costs and makes life more affordable," thanking President Trump for his support.<Cite s="paxton-x-2026-09-05" passage="Protecting the Texas Promise Agenda" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="ap-texas-senate-ad-blitz" kind="checked">
          <p>JT Ennis, a Talarico campaign spokesperson, said in a statement carried by the Associated Press that "Billionaire-backed dark money groups are lying about James Talarico" and that Talarico "was proud to vote for the largest property tax cut in Texas history."<Cite s="ap-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="Billionaire-backed dark money groups are lying about James Talarico" /></p></SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <span class="catch-tag">Told versus record</span>
              <span class="catch-lead">Not a campaign gift.</span>
              CBS wrote that Trump was "donating $10 million" and that "both donations" went to Del Ray Media Inc. The Hill wrote that MAGA Inc. "gave $10 million to the Texas attorney general's Senate campaign." The FEC notice is independent expenditures, not contributions to Ken Paxton for Senate, and Gantt certified they were "not made in cooperation, consultation, or concert with, or at the request or suggestion of, any candidate or authorized committee."<Cite s="cbs-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="donating $10 million" /><Cite s="fec-form24-certification" passage="not made in cooperation, consultation, or concert" />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#claim-check">Claim check</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="catch-tag">Told versus record</span>
              <span class="catch-lead">LLC, not Inc.</span>
              CBS named the vendor "Del Ray Media Inc." The notice names Del Ray Media LLC.<Cite s="cbs-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="Del Ray Media Inc" /><Cite s="fec-form24-2010907" passage="DEL RAY MEDIA LLC" />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#outlets">Coverage</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="catch-tag">Told versus record</span>
              <span class="catch-lead">Not the first outside money.</span>
              The Associated Press called the filing Trump's "first major midterm money move" and Fox News called it MAGA Inc.'s first major midterm buy; neither named the outside money already on file. The FEC independent-expenditure file saved September 8 already lists, for the general election, Lone Star Liberty PAC at ${usd(ieGeneral.loneStarLibertyOpposeTalarico)} opposing Talarico and ${usd(ieGeneral.loneStarLibertySupportPaxton)} supporting Paxton, plus other committees in the millions.<Cite s="fec-ie-2026-bulk" passage="LONE STAR LIBERTY PAC" /><Cite s="ap-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="premier general election contest" />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#where-this-sits">Where this sits</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="catch-tag">Told versus record</span>
              <span class="catch-lead">Not a Graham donation.</span>
              CBS wrote that "MAGA Inc. donated $827,000 to Sen. Darline Graham's campaign." MAGA INC.'s August 24 notice for that South Carolina Senate runoff is independent expenditures totaling ${usd(grahamNotice.ytd)} year-to-date for the office, for text messages and get-out-the-vote phone calls, not a contribution to Graham's campaign committee.<Cite s="cbs-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="donated $827,000" /><Cite s="fec-form24-2009583" passage="Calendar YTD Per Election for Office Sought = $827711.11" />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#outlets">Coverage</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="catch-tag">Left out</span>
              <span class="catch-lead">The 42-39 trial ballot.</span>
              CBS used the Texas Politics Project August poll for a 61 percent Republican job rating for Paxton and did not report that poll's Senate trial ballot: Talarico {poll.talarico} percent, Paxton {poll.paxton} percent, Ted Brown {poll.brown} percent. Democrats broke {poll.demTalarico} percent for Talarico; Republicans {poll.gopPaxton} percent for Paxton; independents {poll.indTalarico} percent Talarico, {poll.indPaxton} percent Paxton, {poll.indNoOpinion} percent no opinion.<Cite s="cbs-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="61% approval rate among Republicans" /><Cite s="ut-txpp-poll-2026-08" passage="42% of Texas voters preferring Democrat James Talarico and 39% planning to vote for Republican Ken Paxton" />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#where-this-sits">Where this sits</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="catch-tag">Who pays</span>
              <span class="catch-lead">Del Ray, not the campaigns.</span>
              MAGA INC. lists two ${usd(filing.opposeTalarico)} expenditures to Del Ray Media LLC. Neither campaign receives the money. Paxton's authorized committees held ${usd(paxton.cashOnHand)} at the end of June.<Cite s="fec-form24-2010907" passage="DEL RAY MEDIA LLC" /><Cite s="fec-paxton-july-quarterly" passage="$1,755,783.35" />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#who-feels-it">Who feels it</a>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="where-this-sits">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The race and the money already on the books</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Where this sits</h2>

        <SourcedBlock source="ap-texas-senate-ad-blitz" kind="checked">
          <p>Ken Paxton is the Texas attorney general and the Republican nominee. "Trump’s endorsement helped Paxton to defeat the incumbent, Sen. John Cornyn, in a May primary runoff election." James Talarico is "a 37-year-old state representative" and the Democratic nominee.<Cite s="ap-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="37-year-old state representative" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Texans for a Conservative Majority reported ${usd(tcm.primaryAndRunoff)} in independent expenditures opposing Paxton across primary and runoff rows in the same FEC bulk file, the money that sat behind that May runoff.<Cite s="fec-ie-2026-bulk" passage="TEXANS FOR A CONSERVATIVE MAJORITY" /></p>

        <SourcedBlock source="sos-goelect-candidates" kind="official">
          <p>The Texas Secretary of State's Candidate Bio Portal, opened in a browser for the 2026 November general election and the U.S. Senate office, lists James Talarico as Democratic, Ken Paxton as Republican, and Ted Brown as Libertarian, each with status "Candidate in the General Election."<Cite s="sos-goelect-candidates" passage="JAMES TALARICO" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="sos-goelect-candidates" kind="official">
          <p>The same capture lists Jade Simmons twice: party Independent, status REJECTED, and party Write-in, status "Candidate in the General Election." It also lists write-in candidates Avery Ayers, Bradford "Brad" Hall, and Zul Mohamed. Simmons is on the Secretary of State's independent-candidate list as rejected; the portal is what shows the write-in filing as accepted.<Cite s="sos-goelect-candidates" passage="STATUS: REJECTED" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="sos-dates-2026" kind="official">
          <p>The Texas Secretary of State's 2026 dates page names Tuesday, November 3, 2026, as general election day, citing Texas Election Code section 41.002.<Cite s="sos-dates-2026" passage="General election day." /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="fec-november-2026-election-date" kind="official">
          <p>The Federal Election Commission likewise lists Tuesday, November 3, 2026, as the next regularly scheduled federal general election date.<Cite s="fec-november-2026-election-date" passage="Tuesday, November 3, 2026" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="sos-advisory-2026-21" kind="official">
          <p>The Secretary of State's November 3 election-law calendar sets a deadline for the office to certify names of party nominees for statewide and district offices to county election officers for that ballot. The calendar extends that deadline to Friday, August 28, 2026.<Cite s="sos-advisory-2026-21" passage="names of party nominees for statewide and district offices" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="sos-independent-declarations-2026" kind="official">
          <p>The Secretary of State's independent-candidate list for 2026 shows Jade Simmons's U.S. Senate filing as rejected. That page is the independent and write-in track, not the party-nominee certification table.<Cite s="sos-independent-declarations-2026" passage="Jade Simmons" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="fec-talarico-july-quarterly" kind="official">
          <p>The FEC candidate page for James Talarico, {filing.talaricoId}, covering September 8, 2025, to June 30, 2026, shows total receipts of ${usd(talarico.receipts)}, including ${usd(talarico.unitemized)} in unitemized individual contributions, {computed.talaricoUnitemizedPct} percent of his individual total. Disbursements through June 30 are ${usd(talarico.disbursements)}. Cash on hand at the end of that period is ${usd(talarico.cashOnHand)}.<Cite s="fec-talarico-july-quarterly" passage="$47,007,775.34" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="fec-paxton-july-quarterly" kind="official">
          <p>The FEC candidate page for Warren Kenneth Paxton Jr., {filing.paxtonId}, covering April 1, 2025, to June 30, 2026, shows total receipts of ${usd(paxton.receipts)} and disbursements of ${usd(paxton.disbursements)}. Cash on hand is ${usd(paxton.cashOnHand)}. Those two receipt totals are {computed.receiptsRatio} to 1. They are not the same start date: Talarico's page starts September 8, 2025; Paxton's starts April 1, 2025.<Cite s="fec-paxton-july-quarterly" passage="$7,492,915.18" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <details class="receipt" data-layer="proof"><summary>how the ratios were counted</summary>
          <p>Talarico receipts ${usd(talarico.receipts)} divided by Paxton receipts ${usd(paxton.receipts)} is {computed.receiptsRatio}. Unitemized Talarico ${usd(talarico.unitemized)} divided by individual ${usd(talarico.individual)} is {computed.talaricoUnitemizedPct} percent. Unitemized Paxton ${usd(paxton.unitemized)} divided by individual ${usd(paxton.individual)} is {computed.paxtonUnitemizedPct} percent. MAGA Inc. ${usd(filing.total)} divided by July 31 cash ${usd(magaCash.cashOnHandEnd)} is {computed.shareOfMagaCashPct} percent. Days from September 5 to November 3 are {computed.daysDisseminationToElection}. Independent-expenditure totals keep the highest file number per committee, candidate, and transaction id in the September 8 bulk file ({ieGeneral.fileRows.toLocaleString("en-US")} rows); {ieGeneral.texasRowsRaw} Texas Senate rows became {ieGeneral.texasRowsDedup} after that step.</p>
        </details>

        <SourcedBlock source="fec-ie-2026-bulk" kind="official">
          <p>The FEC's 2026 independent-expenditure bulk file, saved September 8 ({ieGeneral.fileRows.toLocaleString("en-US")} rows), already shows general-election spending in this race besides MAGA Inc. Lone Star Liberty PAC: ${usd(ieGeneral.loneStarLibertyOpposeTalarico)} opposing Talarico and ${usd(ieGeneral.loneStarLibertySupportPaxton)} supporting Paxton. Truth and Courage PAC: ${usd(ieGeneral.truthAndCourageOpposeTalarico)} opposing Talarico. America PAC: ${usd(ieGeneral.americaPacOpposeTalarico)} opposing Talarico and ${usd(ieGeneral.americaPacSupportPaxton)} supporting Paxton. Lone Star Rising PAC: ${usd(ieGeneral.loneStarRisingOpposePaxton)} opposing Paxton and ${usd(ieGeneral.loneStarRisingSupportTalarico)} supporting Talarico. Protecting America's Future: ${usd(ieGeneral.protectingAmericasFutureOpposeTalarico)} opposing Talarico.<Cite s="fec-ie-2026-bulk" passage="LONE STAR LIBERTY PAC" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="tribune-maga-inc-2026-09-05" kind="checked">
          <p>The Texas Tribune, citing AdImpact, wrote that Musk's America PAC had spent "$1.4 million against Talarico" and that Lone Star Liberty PAC had spent "$18.9 million on the general election thus far." The FEC general-election rows for those committees, recounted above, are lower than the Tribune's Liberty figure. The Tribune also wrote that "Democrats have spent $41.7 million, nearly all from Talarico, to Republicans' $24.3 million," that Senate Leadership Fund "has not put any money into Texas in the general election," that Sen. John Kennedy said he was "hoping that the president's going to spend $100 or $200 million in Texas," and that Sen. Ted Cruz estimated the race could reach $200 million.<Cite s="tribune-maga-inc-2026-09-05" passage="$1.4 million against Talarico" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="fec-maga-inc-august-monthly" kind="official">
          <p>MAGA INC.'s August monthly report covers July 1 through July 31, 2026. Cash on hand at the close of that period is ${usd(magaCash.cashOnHandEnd)}. Receipts in July were ${usd(magaCash.receiptsPeriod)}. Disbursements in July were ${usd(magaCash.disbursementsPeriod)}. The July payee list includes Pfeiffer Public Affairs at ${usd(monthlyPayees.pfeifferPublicAffairs)} and Trump National Golf Club Washington DC at ${usd(monthlyPayees.trumpNationalGolfClub)}. It has no Del Ray Media line.<Cite s="fec-maga-inc-august-monthly" passage="PFEIFFER PUBLIC AFFAIRS" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="fec-ie-2026-bulk" kind="official">
          <p>Del Ray Media LLC is not new to this committee. The same bulk file shows eight MAGA INC. rows to Del Ray in November 2025 for the Tennessee House special election, ${usd(delRay.magaTennesseeNov2025)} in all, and four Congressional Leadership Fund rows to Del Ray dated August 31, 2026, totaling ${usd(delRay.clfAug31)}.<Cite s="fec-ie-2026-bulk" passage="PLACED MEDIA: TV AND CONNECTED TV" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="fec-form24-1952643" kind="record">
          <p>The Texas notice is MAGA INC.'s first 2026 notice coded for the regular November general election. Its other 2026 notices were a March 11 filing coded Special-General for a Georgia 14th District special election, ${usd(marchNotice.total)} in text messages supporting Clay Fuller (two expenditures of ${usd(marchNotice.line)} to Electoral Communications Group, LLC), and the August 24 South Carolina runoff notice for Darline Graham.<Cite s="fec-form24-1952643-csv" passage='"S2026","Special-General"' /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="tribune-maga-inc-2026-09-05" kind="checked">
          <p>Campaigns get a discounted rate from broadcast stations, so "each dollar Talarico spends on those mediums buys more ad time than a GOP PAC," the Tribune wrote, while "MAGA Inc.’s ad buy is for connected television" and digital, "where campaigns and PACs are equal players."<Cite s="tribune-maga-inc-2026-09-05" passage="each dollar Talarico spends on those mediums" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="ut-txpp-poll-2026-08" kind="official">
          <p>The University of Texas / Texas Politics Project August poll, fielded August 5 to 13 among {poll.n.toLocaleString("en-US")} registered voters, found Talarico at {poll.talarico} percent, Paxton at {poll.paxton} percent, Ted Brown at {poll.brown} percent, and {poll.noOpinion} percent with no preference on the trial ballot. The margin of error is plus or minus {poll.moeSample} percent, or {poll.moeWeighted} percent accounting for the weighted sample.<Cite s="ut-txpp-poll-2026-08" passage="The poll was conducted August 5-13, 2026, among 1200 registered voters" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">YouGov interviewed {poll.interviewed.toLocaleString("en-US")} registered voters online from an opt-in panel, matched down to {poll.n.toLocaleString("en-US")}, weighted to demographics and the 2024 presidential vote.<Cite s="ut-txpp-poll-crosstabs" passage="YouGov interviewed 1218 Texas registered voters" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The {computed.pollGapPoints}-point Talarico lead sits inside that weighted margin. Democrats were {poll.demTalarico} percent for Talarico. Republicans were {poll.gopPaxton} percent for Paxton. Independents were {poll.indTalarico} percent Talarico, {poll.indPaxton} percent Paxton, and {poll.indNoOpinion} percent no opinion.<Cite s="ut-txpp-poll-crosstabs" passage="The Republican The Democrat" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The same poll's crosstabs ask how Texans rate Paxton's job as attorney general. Statewide, {poll.approveStrong} percent approve strongly and {poll.approveSomewhat} percent approve somewhat, {computed.paxtonApproveStatewidePct} percent in all; {poll.disapproveSomewhat} percent disapprove somewhat and {poll.disapproveStrong} percent disapprove strongly, {computed.paxtonDisapproveStatewidePct} percent in all. Among Republicans, 29 percent approve strongly and 32 percent approve somewhat, {computed.gopApprovePaxtonPct} percent in all.<Cite s="ut-txpp-poll-crosstabs" passage="How would you rate the job Ken Paxton is doing as" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">CBS wrote that Paxton "survived an impeachment trial in 2023 over allegations of bribery, dereliction of duty and disregard of official duties" and "is still popular among Republicans."<Cite s="cbs-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="survived an impeachment trial in 2023" /></p>

        <SourcedBlock source="nbc-texas-senate-ad-blitz" kind="checked">
          <p>NBC News wrote that Paxton "was impeached by the Republican-led state House in 2023 on allegations of bribery and abuse of public trust, though he was later acquitted by the state Senate after a trial." Senate Majority Leader John Thune "estimated advertising costs $8 million a week" and said Texas "is not one that we can lose."<Cite s="nbc-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="impeached by the Republican-led state House" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="usatoday-maga-inc-2026-09-05" kind="checked">
          <p>USA Today wrote that Cook Political Report and Fox rate the seat a toss-up, and that Trump "plans to disburse between $400 million and $500 million."<Cite s="usatoday-maga-inc-2026-09-05" passage="a toss-up" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="wh-pool-2026-09-04" kind="record">
          <p>The White House in-town pool for September 4 recorded this answer when Trump was asked when the super PAC would spend on the midterms: "We already did. I think I have close to a billion dollars in the Super PAC."<Cite s="wh-pool-2026-09-04" passage="We already did. I think I have close to a billion dollars in the Super PAC." /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The Texas Tribune and USA Today report Trump counting PACs and nonprofits together: the Tribune wrote that he "told reporters Friday that he had close to $1 billion under his control between various PACs and nonprofits, and that he expected to spend $400 million to $500 million on the midterms."<Cite s="tribune-maga-inc-2026-09-05" passage="close to $1 billion under his control" /> MAGA INC. reported ${usd(magaCash.cashOnHandEnd)} on July 31, the cash on the FEC record.<Cite s="fec-maga-inc-august-monthly" passage="403450026.85" /> CBS quoted Trump on August 21 saying "about $850 million," and named "Securing American Greatness, a nonprofit with ties to Mr. Trump's super PAC." A nonprofit does not report its cash to the FEC, so the page can check the billion claim only against the committee's own report. CBS also wrote that Securing American Greatness's ad was a national pro-Trump ad, not one of these Texas spots.<Cite s="cbs-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="about $850 million" /></p>

        <SourcedBlock source="ap-texas-senate-ad-blitz" kind="checked">
          <p>The Associated Press quoted Trump the same day saying, "I'm going to spend whatever amount of money necessary to try and help us," and "This is my money," separate from Republican National Committee funds. The Texas 48-hour notice was signed the next calendar day. The obligation date on the independent-expenditure list is September 4, the same day as the pool report.<Cite s="ap-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="This is my money" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="tribune-maga-inc-2026-09-05" kind="checked">
          <p>The Texas Tribune, citing AdImpact, wrote that Talarico's campaign had spent $40.1 million on general-election advertising and that Paxton's campaign had logged about $264,000, mostly digital. The Tribune transcribed one ad as saying, "Talarico? Higher taxes," and "Paxton? Lower taxes. A clear choice."<Cite s="tribune-maga-inc-2026-09-05" passage="the ad says" /></p></SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="who-feels-it">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Viewers, donors, and the calendar</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Who feels it</h2>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The filing does not say where or to whom the ads run. The purpose line is connected TV and digital advertising. It does not name broadcast stations or zip codes.<Cite s="fec-form24-2010907" passage="PLACED MEDIA: CONNECTED TV & DIGITAL ADVERTISING" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The people who already paid into the race through the campaigns are not the same. Talarico's unitemized individual contributions, the small gifts that do not have to be itemized, are ${usd(talarico.unitemized)} through June 30, {computed.talaricoUnitemizedPct} percent of his individual total. Paxton's unitemized share is {computed.paxtonUnitemizedPct} percent.<Cite s="fec-talarico-july-quarterly" passage="$36,717,034.10" /><Cite s="fec-paxton-july-quarterly" passage="$1,764,118.03" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">MAGA INC. held ${usd(magaCash.cashOnHandEnd)} on July 31. The $10 million is {computed.shareOfMagaCashPct} percent of that pile. It does not move into Paxton's campaign account.<Cite s="fec-maga-inc-august-monthly" passage="403450026.85" /></p>

        <SourcedBlock source="sos-dates-2026" kind="official">
          <p>Early voting by personal appearance for the November 3 general election begins Monday, October 19, 2026.<Cite s="sos-dates-2026" passage="First day of early voting by personal appearance for November 3 general election." /></p></SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="outlets">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Checked against the notice</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What the coverage got right, and what it got wrong</h2>

        <SourcedBlock source="ap-texas-senate-ad-blitz" kind="checked">
          <p>The Associated Press, in a Saturday dispatch from Bridgewater, New Jersey, wrote that the group associated with Trump is spending $10 million on television and digital advertisements "according to a Saturday filing with the Federal Election Commission." The $10 million and the Saturday filing check out. Connected TV is television, so "television and digital" is not a mismatch with the purpose line.<Cite s="ap-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="Saturday filing with the Federal Election Commission" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="nbc-texas-senate-ad-blitz" kind="checked">
          <p>NBC News wrote that MAGA Inc. "had more than $400 million in the bank as of July 31, according to the group's most recent fundraising report." The August monthly close is ${usd(magaCash.cashOnHandEnd)}. That checks out as "more than $400 million."<Cite s="nbc-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="MAGA Inc. had more than $400 million in the bank as of July 31" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="cbs-texas-senate-ad-blitz" kind="checked">
          <p>CBS News got the $5 million / $5 million split and the Saturday filing. It mislabeled the spending as a "donation" and named the vendor "Del Ray Media Inc."<Cite s="cbs-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="Del Ray Media Inc" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="fox-texas-senate-ad-blitz" kind="checked">
          <p>Fox News, published September 5, correctly attributed the $10 million to an FEC filing and carried Paxton's post and Pfeiffer's words. It attributed ${tracker.talaricoSince2025.toFixed(1)} million and ${tracker.paxtonSince2025} million in campaign fundraising to the Texas Tribune. Those figures check out against the Tribune's July 20 fundraising tracker: "raising a cumulative $72.0 million compared to $16.8 million" since the beginning of 2025. They are cumulative from January 2025, which is why they differ from the FEC candidate-page windows through June 30 (${usd(talarico.receipts)} and ${usd(paxton.receipts)}). The September 5 Tribune article on the ads does not carry either figure.<Cite s="fox-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="according to the Texas Tribune" /><Cite s="tribune-tracker-2026-07-20" passage="raising a cumulative $72.0 million compared to $16.8 million" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="thehill-hinojosa-texas-senate" kind="checked">
          <p>The Hill, covering Gina Hinojosa on CNN, wrote that MAGA Inc. "gave $10 million to the Texas attorney general's Senate campaign, per Federal Election Commission (FEC) filings." The filings exist. A gift to the campaign is the wrong legal category.<Cite s="thehill-hinojosa-texas-senate" passage="gave $10 million to the Texas attorney general" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="cnbc-texas-senate-ad-blitz" kind="checked">
          <p>CNBC ran the Associated Press text under its own headline. The same $10 million and Saturday filing check out, with the same "television" wording as the AP copy.<Cite s="cnbc-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="Saturday filing with the Federal Election Commission" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="pbs-texas-senate-ad-blitz" kind="checked">
          <p>PBS NewsHour ran the same Associated Press dispatch. The Saturday filing and $10 million check out. The "television" wording is the AP line again.<Cite s="pbs-texas-senate-ad-blitz" passage="Saturday filing with the Federal Election Commission" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="usatoday-maga-inc-2026-09-05" kind="checked">
          <p>USA Today wrote that MAGA Inc. "has donated $10 million to the campaign" of Paxton. The FEC notice is independent expenditures to Del Ray Media LLC, not a donation to the campaign committee.<Cite s="usatoday-maga-inc-2026-09-05" passage="has donated $10 million to the campaign" /></p></SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="claim-check">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The $10 million</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Claim checked against the record</h2>

        <p data-layer="narrative">This page checks the filing and the outlets' reporting of it. The ads' own claims about Talarico's record are listed but not checked here, because no transcript of the two spots is saved.<Cite s="fec-form24-2010907" passage="PLACED MEDIA: CONNECTED TV & DIGITAL ADVERTISING" /></p>

        <SourcedBlock source="fec-form24-2010907" kind="computed">
          <p>Claim: MAGA Inc. is spending $10 million on Texas Senate ads. The 48-hour notice itemizes ${usd(filing.opposeTalarico)} opposing Talarico and ${usd(filing.supportPaxton)} supporting Paxton. Added, that is ${usd(filing.total)}. The figure is the FEC filing, not an ad-tracking firm's count.<Cite s="fec-form24-2010907" passage="Total Expenditures This Period = $10000000" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <details class="receipt" data-layer="proof"><summary>how this was added</summary>
          <p>Independent-expenditure line opposing Talarico: ${usd(filing.opposeTalarico)}. Independent-expenditure line supporting Paxton: ${usd(filing.supportPaxton)}. 5,000,000 + 5,000,000 = 10,000,000. The notice's own total for the period is ${usd(filing.total)}. Vendor on both lines: Del Ray Media LLC. Purpose on both lines: placed media, connected TV and digital advertising.</p>
        </details>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Claim: this is a contribution to Paxton's campaign. The notice is Form 24, independent expenditures. The payee is a media vendor, not Ken Paxton for Senate. Gantt certified the spending was not made in cooperation with a candidate or authorized committee.<Cite s="fec-form24-2010907" passage="DEL RAY MEDIA LLC" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="what-happened-next">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">After Saturday</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened next</h2>

        <SourcedBlock source="thehill-hinojosa-texas-senate" kind="checked">
          <p>On September 6 The Hill reported Gina Hinojosa, the Democratic nominee for governor, telling CNN's Dana Bash she welcomed the attention on Texas after the $10 million buy in the Senate race.<Cite s="thehill-hinojosa-texas-senate" passage="I welcome the attention on Texas" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="talarico-x-2026-09-05" kind="record">
          <p>Talarico posted the same Saturday: "This is not capitalism; this is crony capitalism." The post does not name the ads, MAGA Inc., Paxton, or Trump. The campaign's response on the record is the Ennis statement carried by the Associated Press.<Cite s="talarico-x-2026-09-05" passage="This is not capitalism; this is crony capitalism." /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Watching: MAGA INC.'s next 24-hour or 48-hour notices, the candidates' October quarterly reports due October 15 (books close September 30), the pre-general reports due October 22 (books close October 14), and early voting beginning October 19.<Cite s="fec-2026-quarterly-filers" passage="10/15/2026" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="unknowns">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Open questions</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What we do not know yet</h2>
        <ul data-layer="fact">
          <li>Broadcast-TV reservations, if any, beyond the connected-TV and digital purpose line on the notice, and AdImpact's underlying table rather than the Tribune's quotation of it.<Cite s="fec-form24-2010907" passage="PLACED MEDIA: CONNECTED TV & DIGITAL ADVERTISING" /></li>
          <li>The candidates' fundraising after June 30. Their next quarterly reports are due October 15. A pre-general report closes October 14 and is due October 22.<Cite s="fec-2026-quarterly-filers" passage="10/15/2026" /></li>
        </ul>
      </section>

